[{'id': 21048655, 'title': None, 'body': '<p>If you will not use strongSelf in the code suggested by Kevin Ballard then it might lead to a crash because of weak getting nilled out.</p>\n\n<p>Also a good practice would be to even check for strong being non nil at the point of creating      </p>\n\n<pre><code>strongSelf = weakSelf\n\n   if(strongSelf)\n   {\n       // do your stuff here \n   }\n</code></pre>', 'accepted_answer_id': None, 'answer_count': None, 'comment_count': 0, 'community_owned_date': 'NaT', 'creation_date': '2014-01-10 15:53:33.090000+00:00', 'favorite_count': None, 'last_activity_date': '2014-01-10 15:53:33.090000+00:00', 'last_edit_date': 'NaT', 'last_editor_display_name': None, 'last_editor_user_id': None, 'owner_display_name': None, 'owner_user_id': 1785418, 'parent_id': 14370325, 'post_type_id': 2, 'score': 0, 'tags': None, 'view_count': None}, {'id': 21048662, 'title': None, 'body': '<p>Every Draw object creates a new timer so you have the game running n times at the same time but the copies of the game share the state because ir is stored in static variables so the state is updated n-1 times faster than it should.</p>\n\n<p>You may stop the timer <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/1409116/how-to-stop-the-task-scheduled-in-java-util-timer-class">this way</a> or you may use a static timer and <a href="http://docs.oracle.com/javase/1.5.0/docs/api/java/util/Timer.html#schedule%28java.util.TimerTask,%20long,%20long%29" rel="nofollow noreferrer">schedule/cancel the task</a> each time.</p>', 'accepted_answer_id': None, 'answer_count': None, 'comment_count': 0, 'community_owned_date': 'NaT', 'creation_date': '2014-01-10 15:53:51.817000+00:00', 'favorite_count': None, 'last_activity_date': '2014-01-10 15:53:51.817000+00:00', 'last_edit_date': '2017-05-23 12:21:28.453000+00:00', 'last_editor_display_name': None, 'last_editor_user_id': -1, 'owner_display_name': None, 'owner_user_id': 270349, 'parent_id': 21048393, 'post_type_id': 2, 'score': 0, 'tags': None, 'view_count': None}, {'id': 21048666, 'title': None, 'body': '<pre><code>a=[["A" for i in range(4)] for j in range(4)]\n\nfor i in range(len(a)):\n  print()\n  for j in a[i]:\n     print("%c "%j,end=\'\')\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>it will print like this:</p>\n\n<pre><code>A A A A\nA A A A\nA A A A\nA A A A\n</code></pre>', 'accepted_answer_id': None, 'answer_count': None, 'comment_count': 0, 'community_owned_date': 'NaT', 'creation_date': '2014-01-10 15:54:03.450000+00:00', 'favorite_count': None, 'last_activity_date': '2014-01-10 16:20:00.777000+00:00', 'last_edit_date': '2014-01-10 16:20:00.777000+00:00', 'last_editor_display_name': None, 'last_editor_user_id': 1887779, 'owner_display_name': None, 'owner_user_id': 3182465, 'parent_id': 16541973, 'post_type_id': 2, 'score': 0, 'tags': None, 'view_count': None}, {'id': 21048669, 'title': None, 'body': '<p>Using standard library:\n</p>\n\n<pre><code>&gt;&gt;&gt; from itertools import groupby \n&gt;&gt;&gt; groups = [list(g[1]) for g in groupby(a, lambda x: x &gt; 44)]\n&gt;&gt;&gt; print groups\n\n[[380.01874, 50.551693], [42.776562, 42.650467, 42.439999, 42.408905, \n42.260563, 42.108276, 42.168007, 42.118984, 42.124863, 42.043224, \n42.095177, 41.915165, 42.06472, 41.938156, 41.893482, 41.941589, \n41.8997, 41.925949, 41.819122, 41.86272, 41.75209, 41.802406, 41.760361], \n[45.260254, 46.825336, 46.500366, 44.3283], [41.763329, 41.780571, \n41.672211, 41.641739, 41.651295, 41.618404, 41.695488, 41.671284, \n41.621643, 41.441597, 41.66975, 41.577599, 41.615841, 41.495602, \n41.561581, 41.560726, 41.6759, 41.537495, 41.401688, 41.618671, \n41.560268, 41.503071, 41.528313], [45.30022, 48.274105], [41.432148, \n41.471291, 41.512859, 41.50565, 41.512371, 41.466312, 41.473175, \n41.515701, 41.500916, 41.439285, 41.492058, 41.4678, 41.40226, \n41.388351, 41.381634, 41.41087, 41.469292, 41.360497, 41.5056, 41.378258, \n41.513493, 41.330299, 41.396099, 41.402149, 41.358822], [44.038559, \n47.995853, 48.233227, 44.078781], [41.41098, 41.243141, 41.356258, \n41.455688, 41.244099, 41.360558, 41.313278, 41.463379, 41.245998, \n41.313683, 41.220814, 41.380154, 41.345943, 41.313438, 41.259727, \n41.361221, 41.238659, 41.397285]]\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>It is then straightforward to group this list into pairs if that\'s what you need:</p>\n\n<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>pairs = zip(*[iter(groups)] * 2)\n</code></pre>\n\n<p>The result would be a a list of 2-tuples, each one consisting of two lists (values greater than 44 and values less than 44).</p>', 'accepted_answer_id': None, 'answer_count': None, 'comment_count': 0, 'community_owned_date': 'NaT', 'creation_date': '2014-01-10 15:54:06.590000+00:00', 'favorite_count': None, 'last_activity_date': '2014-01-10 15:54:06.590000+00:00', 'last_edit_date': 'NaT', 'last_editor_display_name': None, 'last_editor_user_id': None, 'owner_display_name': None, 'owner_user_id': 1115659, 'parent_id': 21045041, 'post_type_id': 2, 'score': 0, 'tags': None, 'view_count': None}, {'id': 21048684, 'title': None, 'body': '<p><code>time</code> function and <code>time_t</code> type granularity is usually seconds, so if your function returns in less than 1 second the diff will be zero.\nFor quick and dirty benchmarking you can use <code>clock()</code>.</p>', 'accepted_answer_id': None, 'answer_count': None, 'comment_count': 0, 'community_owned_date': 'NaT', 'creation_date': '2014-01-10 15:54:40.657000+00:00', 'favorite_count': None, 'last_activity_date': '2014-01-10 15:54:40.657000+00:00', 'last_edit_date': 'NaT', 'last_editor_display_name': None, 'last_editor_user_id': None, 'owner_display_name': None, 'owner_user_id': 2382136, 'parent_id': 21048465, 'post_type_id': 2, 'score': 0, 'tags': None, 'view_count': None}]

